=========================================================
FUNCTION TEMPLATES (C++11/14/17)
Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is a Function Template?
===============================

A function template allows writing a single generic function that can work
with different data types.

Instead of writing separate functions for int, float, double, string, etc.,
we write one generic implementation.

Example (Without Templates)

int max(int a, int b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

double max(double a, double b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

string max(string a, string b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

Problem:
--------
• Duplicate code
• Difficult to maintain

---------------------------------------------------------

Using Function Templates

template<typename T>
T max(T a, T b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

Usage

cout << max(10,20);
cout << max(3.5,4.2);
cout << max(string("ABC"), string("XYZ"));

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Syntax
=========

template<typename T>
ReturnType functionName(parameters)
{
    // implementation
}

Example

template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

3. How Function Templates Work
==============================

Templates are compile-time blueprints.

The compiler generates the required function automatically when the template
is used.

Example

template<typename T>
T square(T x)
{
    return x * x;
}

Calls

square(5);        // T = int
square(2.5);      // T = double

Compiler Generates

int square(int);

double square(double);

This process is called:

Template Instantiation

---------------------------------------------------------

4. Template Type Deduction
==========================

The compiler usually deduces the template type automatically.

Example

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

Calls

print(10);          // T = int
print(3.14);        // T = double
print("Hello");     // T = const char*

No need to specify T explicitly.

---------------------------------------------------------

5. Explicit Template Arguments
==============================

Sometimes we explicitly specify the template type.

Example

template<typename T>
void display(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

display<int>(100);

Equivalent to

display(100);

Useful when type deduction fails or when you want to force a type.

---------------------------------------------------------

6. Multiple Template Parameters
===============================

A function template can have more than one template parameter.

Example

template<typename T, typename U>
auto add(T a, U b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Usage

add(10,5.5);

Compiler Deduces

T = int
U = double

Return Type = double

---------------------------------------------------------

7. Non-Type Template Parameters
===============================

Templates can also accept constant values.

Example

template<typename T, int SIZE>
void printArray(T (&arr)[SIZE])
{
    for(const auto& x : arr)
        cout << x << " ";
}

Usage

int arr[] = {1,2,3,4};

printArray(arr);

Compiler Deduces

T = int
SIZE = 4

---------------------------------------------------------

8. Default Template Arguments
=============================

Template parameters can have default values.

Example

template<typename T = int>
T multiply(T a, T b)
{
    return a * b;
}

multiply(2,3);

Compiler Uses

T = int

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Template Function Overloading
================================

Function templates can be overloaded.

Example

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

template<typename T>
void print(T a, T b)
{
    cout << a << " " << b;
}

Calls

print(10);
print(10,20);

---------------------------------------------------------

10. Normal Function vs Function Template
========================================

Example

void print(int)
{
    cout << "Normal Function";
}

template<typename T>
void print(T)
{
    cout << "Template Function";
}

Calls

print(10);      // Normal Function

print(3.5);     // Template Function

Rule

If both match equally well, the compiler prefers the non-template function.

---------------------------------------------------------

11. Function Template with Auto Return Type (C++14)
===================================================

template<typename T, typename U>
auto multiply(T a, U b)
{
    return a * b;
}

Usage

multiply(5,2.5);

Return Type

double

---------------------------------------------------------

12. Trailing Return Type (C++11)
================================

Useful when the return type depends on template parameters.

Example

template<typename T, typename U>
auto add(T a, U b) -> decltype(a + b)
{
    return a + b;
}

---------------------------------------------------------

13. Function Template Limitations
=================================

The operations used inside the template must be valid for that type.

Example

template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

This works only if operator+ is defined for T.

---------------------------------------------------------

14. Advantages
==============

